1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,512
The Mystery of Black Holes.

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:14,337
A black hole is a region of spacetime where gravitational acceleration is so extreme that nothing—not even electromagnetic radiation—can escape beyond a boundary called the event horizon.

3
00:00:14,337 --> 00:00:23,700
The Schwarzschild solution to Einstein's field equations, published by Karl Schwarzschild in 1916, first predicted this phenomenon mathematically.

4
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:26,775
Black holes form through several pathways.

5
00:00:26,775 --> 00:00:43,800
Stellar-mass black holes (typically 5 to 20 solar masses) arise when massive stars—those exceeding approximately 20 solar masses—exhaust their nuclear fuel and undergo core collapse, compressing the core beyond the neutron star density limit into a singularity.

6
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:59,850
Supermassive black holes, with masses ranging from millions to billions of solar masses, inhabit the centers of most large galaxies, including Sagittarius A* at the center of the Milky Way (approximately 4.3 million solar masses).

7
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:05,337
The formation mechanisms for supermassive black holes remain an active research area.

8
00:01:05,337 --> 00:01:08,775
The event horizon marks the point of no return.

9
00:01:08,775 --> 00:01:14,675
From the perspective of a distant observer, time dilation approaches infinity at this boundary.

10
00:01:14,675 --> 00:01:21,912
At the center lies the singularity, where spacetime curvature becomes infinite and classical physics ceases to apply.

11
00:01:21,912 --> 00:01:34,425
In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope—a planet-scale very-long-baseline interferometry network linking radio observatories across Earth—captured the first direct image of a black hole's shadow.

12
00:01:34,425 --> 00:01:52,650
The image revealed the supermassive black hole at the center of galaxy M87 (M87*), approximately 55 million light-years distant, with a dark central region 2.5 times larger than the event horizon itself, consistent with general relativistic predictions.

13
00:01:52,650 --> 00:02:03,325
In 2022, the same collaboration imaged Sagittarius A* in polarized light, revealing the magnetic field structure around the Milky Way's central black hole.

14
00:02:03,325 --> 00:02:19,025
The Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory (LIGO) detected the first gravitational waves from merging stellar-mass black holes in 2015 (GW150914), inaugurating gravitational-wave astronomy.

15
00:02:19,025 --> 00:02:34,225
Stephen Hawking's 1974 prediction of Hawking radiation—quantum emission from black hole event horizons—linked general relativity to quantum field theory, though this radiation is negligible for any black hole above a few solar masses.

16
00:02:34,225 --> 00:02:46,312
The black hole information paradox, concerning whether matter and energy information falling into a black hole is permanently lost, remains one of the deepest unsolved problems in theoretical physics.

